From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Carlos Chulo <cchulo.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 10:00:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] HID: asus-ally: do not touch the other interface's state on
 remove

The driver binds two USB interfaces of the N-KEY device: the
configuration interface (endpoint ROG_ALLY_CFG_INTF_IN, which creates
drvdata.gamepad_cfg and drvdata.led_rgb_dev) and the Ally X gamepad
interface (ROG_ALLY_X_INTF_IN, which creates drvdata.ally_x). All three
objects are devm allocations on their own interface's hid_device, so
each is freed right after that interface's remove() returns. drvdata is
a single static shared by both interfaces, and nothing clears its
pointers.

ally_hid_remove() tears down every part on every interface:

	if (drvdata.led_rgb_dev) ally_rgb_remove(hdev);
	if (drvdata.ally_x)      ally_x_remove(hdev);
	if (drvdata.gamepad_cfg) ally_cfg_remove(hdev);

On rmmod, driver_detach() unbinds the device bound last first. When the
Ally X interface goes first, its devres frees ally_x (and its input
device, which is when userspace sees "No such device"). The
configuration interface is still bound, and two things then use freed
memory:

- ally_raw_event() on the configuration interface still reads
  drvdata.ally_x. Every 0x5a vendor report goes to ally_x_raw_event()
  on the freed object: input_report_key() on a freed input_dev, its
  spinlock, schedule_work() on a freed work item. This runs in URB
  completion (interrupt) context.
- The configuration interface's remove() then calls ally_x_remove() on
  the freed object: spin_lock_irqsave() and cancel_work_sync() on freed
  memory.

When the configuration interface goes first, ally_x_remove() runs
against the still-bound gamepad interface and cancels its rumble
worker. ally_cfg_remove() later removes the gamepad attribute groups
from the wrong kobject. The probe path has related problems:

- drvdata.ally_x briefly holds an ERR_PTR that raw_event can read.
- drvdata.led_rgb_dev and drvdata.gamepad_cfg stay ERR_PTRs on failure,
  but suspend, resume and remove only test them for NULL.
- The Ally X probe unregisters gamepad_cfg->input without clearing it,
  so the configuration path of raw_event reports into a freed input
  device once ally_x is gone.

Whether a stale access crashes depends on whether the freed memory has
been reused, so a live "modprobe -r hid_asus_ally" can succeed several
times and then take the machine down. The interrupt-context accesses
leave no oops in the journal. This was seen on a ROG Xbox Ally X20 while
InputPlumber held the gamepad evdev: an immediate hard reboot, nothing
logged after InputPlumber's "event11: No such device", and nothing in
pstore. The crash is not proven to be this bug, but the code above is
wrong either way.

Fix:

- remove() tears down only the parts its own interface created
  (selected by endpoint, as probe does) and clears the drvdata pointer
  before devres frees the object.
- A spinlock, ally_drvdata_lock, serialises raw_event's reads of
  drvdata.ally_x, drvdata.gamepad_cfg and gamepad_cfg->input against
  probe and remove changing them. raw_event dispatches under the lock,
  so a pointer cannot be freed while a report is being handled.
- probe never publishes an ERR_PTR: a failed part is NULL.
- The Ally X probe clears gamepad_cfg->input before unregistering it.
- ally_x_play_effect() returns early when drvdata.ally_x is already
  cleared. The ff device goes away with the input device, after
  remove() returns.

Audit of the LED paths added by the earlier patches in this series
(no defect found):

- ally_rgb_set() (the LED core's brightness_set, possibly atomic) only
  takes led->lock and schedules work. It never calls
  asus_dev_set_report().
- ally_rgb_remove() sets led->removed under the lock, then calls
  cancel_work_sync(), then unregisters the class device. The LED_OFF
  that led_classdev_unregister() sends goes to ally_rgb_set(), and
  ally_rgb_schedule_work() refuses to queue once removed is set. No HID
  I/O happens after cancel_work_sync(). The effect/speed/direction/
  enabled stores are kernfs attributes removed before unregistering,
  and they queue work only through the same guarded helper.
- The "0xb4 only on effect change" logic and all Aura I/O run in the
  work item (process context).
- The rgb_static_mode parameter's set callback only validates and
  stores an int. The work reads it with READ_ONCE(). Nothing touches a
  device.
- This matches hid-playstation, which clears output_worker_initialized
  under the lock, cancels the worker and leaves the devm LEDs to devres.
  Their brightness_set callbacks only schedule work, which is refused
  after teardown.

Derived from: drivers/hid/hid-playstation.c (ps_remove(),
dualsense_schedule_work(): flag under lock, cancel_work_sync(), devm
LEDs) and drivers/hid/hid-nintendo.c (nintendo_hid_remove(): output
worker stopped before teardown) in the same tree; the driver-core
detach order in drivers/base/dd.c (driver_detach() takes the last bound
device first).

Base: linux-integration 7.2.7-valve2 plus the previous patches in this
series.

TODO(hw): on an RC74XA with InputPlumber running, repeat
"modprobe -r hid_asus_ally && modprobe hid-asus-ally" (at least ten
times) and check that it neither reboots nor logs a KASAN or oops
report.
---
 drivers/hid/hid-asus-ally.c | 112 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++---------
 1 file changed, 86 insertions(+), 26 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/hid/hid-asus-ally.c b/drivers/hid/hid-asus-ally.c
index cd45ebc..e46962c 100644
--- a/drivers/hid/hid-asus-ally.c
+++ b/drivers/hid/hid-asus-ally.c
@@ -535,6 +535,17 @@ static struct ally_drvdata {
 	uint mcu_version;
 } drvdata;
 
+/*
+ * drvdata is shared by the configuration interface (gamepad_cfg, led_rgb_dev)
+ * and the Ally X gamepad interface (ally_x). Each part lives in devres memory
+ * of the interface that created it and is freed right after that interface's
+ * remove() returns, while the other interface can still be bound and still
+ * receiving reports. Readers in raw_event (interrupt context) and the
+ * writers in probe/remove take this lock, so a pointer is never used after
+ * its owner cleared it.
+ */
+static DEFINE_SPINLOCK(ally_drvdata_lock);
+
 static void reverse_bytes_in_pairs(u8 *buf, size_t size) {
 	uint16_t *word_ptr;
 	size_t i;
@@ -1637,10 +1648,11 @@ static struct input_dev *ally_x_alloc_input_dev(struct hid_device *hdev,
 
 static int ally_x_play_effect(struct input_dev *idev, void *data, struct ff_effect *effect)
 {
-	struct ally_x_device *ally_x = drvdata.ally_x;
+	struct ally_x_device *ally_x = READ_ONCE(drvdata.ally_x);
 	unsigned long flags;
 
-	if (effect->type != FF_RUMBLE)
+	/* NULL once the interface is being removed; the input device goes next */
+	if (!ally_x || effect->type != FF_RUMBLE)
 		return 0;
 
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&ally_x->lock, flags);
@@ -2630,8 +2642,14 @@ static void ally_rgb_remove(struct hid_device *hdev)
 static int ally_raw_event(struct hid_device *hdev, struct hid_report *report, u8 *data,
 			  int size)
 {
-	struct ally_gamepad_cfg *cfg = drvdata.gamepad_cfg;
-	struct ally_x_device *ally_x = drvdata.ally_x;
+	struct ally_gamepad_cfg *cfg;
+	struct ally_x_device *ally_x;
+	unsigned long flags;
+	int ret = 0;
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&ally_drvdata_lock, flags);
+	cfg = drvdata.gamepad_cfg;
+	ally_x = drvdata.ally_x;
 
 	if (ally_x) {
 		if ((hdev->bus == BUS_USB && report->id == ALLY_X_INPUT_REPORT_USB &&
@@ -2639,11 +2657,9 @@ static int ally_raw_event(struct hid_device *hdev, struct hid_report *report, u8
 		    (data[0] == 0x5A)) {
 			ally_x_raw_event(ally_x, report, data, size);
 		} else {
-			return -1;
+			ret = -1;
 		}
-	}
-
-	if (cfg && !ally_x) {
+	} else if (cfg && cfg->input) {
 		input_report_key(cfg->input, KEY_PROG1, data[1] == 0x38);
 		input_report_key(cfg->input, KEY_PROG2, data[1] == ALLY_X20_KEY_RIGHT);
 		input_report_key(cfg->input, KEY_F16, data[1] == 0xA6);
@@ -2651,8 +2667,9 @@ static int ally_raw_event(struct hid_device *hdev, struct hid_report *report, u8
 		input_report_key(cfg->input, KEY_F18, data[1] == 0xA8);
 		input_sync(cfg->input);
 	}
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&ally_drvdata_lock, flags);
 
-	return 0;
+	return ret;
 }
 
 static int ally_hid_init(struct hid_device *hdev)
@@ -2694,6 +2711,10 @@ static int ally_hid_probe(struct hid_device *hdev, const struct hid_device_id *_
 	struct usb_interface *intf = to_usb_interface(hdev->dev.parent);
 	struct usb_device *udev = interface_to_usbdev(intf);
 	u16 idProduct = le16_to_cpu(udev->descriptor.idProduct);
+	struct ally_gamepad_cfg *cfg;
+	struct ally_x_device *ally_x;
+	struct ally_rgb_dev *led;
+	unsigned long flags;
 	int ret, ep;
 
 	ep = get_endpoint_address(hdev);
@@ -2735,35 +2756,56 @@ static int ally_hid_probe(struct hid_device *hdev, const struct hid_device_id *_
 		validate_mcu_fw_version(hdev, idProduct);
 		ally_disable_nkey_wakeup(hdev);
 
-		drvdata.led_rgb_dev = ally_rgb_create(hdev);
-		if (IS_ERR(drvdata.led_rgb_dev))
+		/* Keep NULL, never an ERR_PTR, in drvdata: every user tests for NULL */
+		led = ally_rgb_create(hdev);
+		if (IS_ERR(led)) {
 			hid_err(hdev, "Failed to create Ally gamepad LEDs.\n");
-		else
+			led = NULL;
+		} else {
 			hid_info(hdev, "Created Ally RGB LED controls.\n");
+		}
+		drvdata.led_rgb_dev = led;
 
-		drvdata.gamepad_cfg = ally_gamepad_cfg_create(hdev);
-		if (IS_ERR(drvdata.gamepad_cfg))
+		/* ally_gamepad_cfg_create() sets drvdata.gamepad_cfg itself */
+		cfg = ally_gamepad_cfg_create(hdev);
+		if (IS_ERR(cfg)) {
 			hid_err(hdev, "Failed to create Ally gamepad attributes.\n");
-		else
+			cfg = NULL;
+		} else {
 			hid_info(hdev, "Created Ally gamepad attributes.\n");
+		}
+		spin_lock_irqsave(&ally_drvdata_lock, flags);
+		drvdata.gamepad_cfg = cfg;
+		spin_unlock_irqrestore(&ally_drvdata_lock, flags);
 
-		if (IS_ERR(drvdata.led_rgb_dev) && IS_ERR(drvdata.gamepad_cfg))
+		if (!led && !cfg)
 			goto err_close;
 	}
 
 	/* May or may not exist */
 	if (ep == ROG_ALLY_X_INTF_IN) {
-		drvdata.ally_x = ally_x_create(hdev);
-		if (IS_ERR(drvdata.ally_x)) {
+		/* raw_event may run already: never expose an ERR_PTR to it */
+		ally_x = ally_x_create(hdev);
+		if (IS_ERR(ally_x)) {
 			hid_err(hdev, "Failed to create Ally X gamepad.\n");
-			drvdata.ally_x = NULL;
 			goto err_close;
 		}
+		spin_lock_irqsave(&ally_drvdata_lock, flags);
+		drvdata.ally_x = ally_x;
+		spin_unlock_irqrestore(&ally_drvdata_lock, flags);
 		hid_info(hdev, "Created Ally X controller.\n");
 
 		// Not required since we send this inputs ep through the gamepad input dev
 		if (drvdata.gamepad_cfg && drvdata.gamepad_cfg->input) {
-			input_unregister_device(drvdata.gamepad_cfg->input);
+			struct input_dev *input;
+
+			/* raw_event must not use it once it is unregistered (and freed) */
+			spin_lock_irqsave(&ally_drvdata_lock, flags);
+			input = drvdata.gamepad_cfg->input;
+			drvdata.gamepad_cfg->input = NULL;
+			spin_unlock_irqrestore(&ally_drvdata_lock, flags);
+
+			input_unregister_device(input);
 			hid_info(hdev, "Ally X removed unrequired input dev.\n");
 		}
 	}
@@ -2777,16 +2819,34 @@ err_stop:
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Tear down only what this interface created, then clear the drvdata pointer
+ * before devres frees the object. The two interfaces unbind one after the
+ * other (on rmmod the one bound last goes first), and the other interface's
+ * remove() and raw_event must not touch the freed parts.
+ */
 static void ally_hid_remove(struct hid_device *hdev)
 {
-	if (drvdata.led_rgb_dev)
-		ally_rgb_remove(hdev);
+	unsigned long flags;
+	int ep = get_endpoint_address(hdev);
 
-	if (drvdata.ally_x)
+	if (ep == ROG_ALLY_CFG_INTF_IN) {
+		if (drvdata.led_rgb_dev)
+			ally_rgb_remove(hdev);
+		drvdata.led_rgb_dev = NULL;
+
+		if (drvdata.gamepad_cfg)
+			ally_cfg_remove(hdev);
+		spin_lock_irqsave(&ally_drvdata_lock, flags);
+		drvdata.gamepad_cfg = NULL;
+		spin_unlock_irqrestore(&ally_drvdata_lock, flags);
+	} else if (ep == ROG_ALLY_X_INTF_IN && drvdata.ally_x) {
+		/* Still allocated until this function returns */
 		ally_x_remove(hdev);
-
-	if (drvdata.gamepad_cfg)
-		ally_cfg_remove(hdev);
+		spin_lock_irqsave(&ally_drvdata_lock, flags);
+		drvdata.ally_x = NULL;
+		spin_unlock_irqrestore(&ally_drvdata_lock, flags);
+	}
 
 	hid_hw_close(hdev);
 	hid_hw_stop(hdev);
